Experiment 2: Link a Local Repository with GitHub and Push Code

Aim

To connect a local Git repository with a remote GitHub repository and upload files using Git commands.

Requirements

- Git installed on the computer
- GitHub account
- Local repository
- Internet connection

Procedure

1. Create a project folder and initialize Git.
2. Create a file and add it to the staging area.
3. Commit the changes.
4. Connect the local repository to the remote GitHub repository.
5. Rename the current branch to "main".
6. Push the code to GitHub.
7. Verify the uploaded file in the GitHub repository.

Commands

mkdir MyProject
cd MyProject
git init
echo "Hello GitHub" > index.txt
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git remote add origin https://github.com/Sudiksha784/My-First-Git-Project.git
git branch -M main
git push -u origin main

Explanation of Commands

- "mkdir MyProject" – Creates a project folder.
- "cd MyProject" – Opens the project folder.
- "git init" – Initializes Git.
- "echo "Hello GitHub" > index.txt" – Creates a text file.
- "git add ." – Stages all changes in the current folder.
- "git commit -m "Initial commit"" – Commits the staged changes.
- "git remote add origin URL" – Connects the local repository to GitHub.
- "git branch -M main" – Renames the current branch to "main".
- "git push -u origin main" – Uploads the branch to GitHub and sets its upstream.

Expected Output

The local repository is connected to GitHub, and the committed files are uploaded successfully.

Result

Thus, a local Git repository was linked with a remote GitHub repository, and the code was pushed successfully.

Important Note

The commands above assume that the GitHub repository is empty or can accept the initial push without conflicts. If the remote repository already contains files or commits, you may need to clone it or pull and reconcile the changes first. If authentication is requested, sign in using an approved Git credential method; never put your password or access token directly in the commands.